#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f0307723-f211-541e-96c0-e4212c9ecf32" :question/math-academy-id "e-2483" :question/problem "Is the vector function $\\mathbf f(t) = -\\sin t \\,\\mathbf i + (t^2 + 1)\\,\\mathbf j + \\tan t\\,\\mathbf k$ continuous at $t = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the limit of $\\mathbf f (t)$ as $t$ approaches $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}+1),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{0}^{2}+1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0)$ and therefore $\\mathbf f (t)$ is continuous at $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114975 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "e08c55e9-d21c-5a33-890c-509847fbbbe8" :question/math-academy-id "e-2484" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf g(1)$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $\\mathbf f(1) = \\left\\langle 1, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =2{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)-g(1) \\\\  & =2{{⟨1,1,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(1)}-g(1) \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1) & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d2650d6c-2bbf-5f1e-bed4-dbba454334cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2485" :question/problem "Is the vector function $\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\left\\langle 2e^t, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{t-3}, \\: \\sqrt{t} \\right\\rangle$ differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t \\in [0, 2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is defined on $t \\in [0, 2].$ Remember that a function is differentiable on the interval $[0, 2]$ if and only if all of its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now differentiate $\\mathbf f(t)$ to find $\\mathbf f'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi }{t-3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{(t-3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the third component of this vector, $\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}$ is undefined at $t = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is **not** differentiable on $[0,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74401a99-8151-5848-bfcc-1eef2569c204" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval on Which a Product of Functions is Differentiable"}] :question/id #uuid "0b25dc9c-70f3-5be6-87d1-cf2a29dd39e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2486" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $\\mathbf g(t)\\cdot\\mathbf h(t)$ is differentiable,  given the following functions $\\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf h(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j-\\sqrt{t}\\,k \\\\ h(t) & =2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F(t) = \\mathbf g(t)\\cdot\\mathbf h(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $\\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf h(t)$ are differentiable. Remember that a vector function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nSo, let's start by finding the intervals where each of the functions $\\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf h(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $\\mathbf g(t)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- The first component, $3\\sin t,$ and  the second component, $-\\cos(2t),$ are  differentiable over $(-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- The third component $- \\sqrt t$ is differentiable on $(0, \\infty).$ Its derivative is not defined at $t=0$ and the function is not defined for $t <0.$\n\nThe intersection of the intervals of differentiability of all three components of $\\mathbf g(t)$ is $(0,\\infty).$  So, $\\mathbf g(t)$ is differentiable on $(0,\\infty).$\n\nFor the function $\\mathbf h(t)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- All three component functions are defined and are differentiable for all values of $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$ \n\nSo, $\\mathbf h(t)$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $\\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf h(t)$  is $(0, \\infty) \\cap (-\\infty, \\infty) =  (0,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f216b60-e388-51f5-ba21-e8378f9d2558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(s)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563163} {:answer/id #uuid "897e666e-5017-5c68-b94e-f2a61cddac86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the functions are continuous on $[-1,1]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563250} {:answer/id #uuid "8d44025a-769b-53ed-8380-a28de619549a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(s)$  and $q(s)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563264} {:answer/id #uuid "98a35e8c-50cb-52af-93af-e4c92463935b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563287} {:answer/id #uuid "ea628c3b-5f80-5519-9722-358822a7671c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q(s)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897e666e-5017-5c68-b94e-f2a61cddac86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the functions are continuous on $[-1,1]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563250} :answer-field/id #uuid "e733f8a2-e030-5a6d-ae1a-9d5ff92e104f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48e5a226-dec1-538c-a739-b2da6b892931" :question/math-academy-id "q-127807" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous on $[-1,1]?$\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(s) & =|{s}^{2}-1|\\,i+\\frac{s+1}{s}\\,j+2{s}^{2}\\,k \\\\ q(s) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)\\,i+({s}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,k \\\\ r(s) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\,i-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡s\\,j+\\operatorname{csc}⁡s\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.  \n\nLet's consider the functions one by one. \n\n- The function $p(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits second component, $\\frac{s+1}{s},$ is undefined at $s=0$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function $q(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits first component, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)$, is undefined at $s=1$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function $r(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits third component, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡s$, is undefined at $s=0$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions are continuous on $[-1,1].$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4f36e5-b04e-5bd5-a6fb-83da272ae33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562974} {:answer/id #uuid "2992adf6-cddf-5fc4-9787-9ba131d5b5d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563038} {:answer/id #uuid "608e2ed1-b00d-5789-b147-2890b9a3778f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563168} {:answer/id #uuid "6982b8e4-6126-5d97-ba8a-51a2aac2d0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563189} {:answer/id #uuid "ebfed41d-7c24-5273-bbf1-93cf15a6a3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebfed41d-7c24-5273-bbf1-93cf15a6a3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563473} :answer-field/id #uuid "32f815ec-32d1-5da6-a873-03e986d0bb8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd43e1d4-a6c0-5d8f-9dc6-6b6b8e30eb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-127809" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2{e}^{t-1},\\,\\sqrt{5-t}⟩$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $2{e}^{t-1}$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{5-t}$ is continuous for $t\\le 5.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for $t\\le 5.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2e53bc-1e51-5158-be37-150f2d6da11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563007} {:answer/id #uuid "4a83e165-b269-52f5-a197-caf273f7ea71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563107} {:answer/id #uuid "5010e8d0-e598-5d84-91f0-984ce80e6324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563128} {:answer/id #uuid "de7f6515-a2ae-5122-8741-2cd8e6ab4ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563432} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ae7443-43d6-5655-96bf-fbf2d09a5527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c2e53bc-1e51-5158-be37-150f2d6da11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563007} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aec6766-90e2-5088-80c3-02f015d74372" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b1d012f-068b-5ef8-8288-effe2d51246e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127810" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{aligned}]$  is continuous." :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded points $t=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{t}$ is continuous for $t\\ge 0.$\n\n- The function $t+3$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for all $t\\ge 0$ except the point $t=1.$ So the interval of continuity is $[0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04142752-c01a-5e11-89a6-6b5adc4ed5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-2,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562945} {:answer/id #uuid "21d9e57e-bfa3-586e-9b91-d4dc21a2545d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563020} {:answer/id #uuid "24d7bfc2-51e1-5033-adbd-48343a267821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563028} {:answer/id #uuid "2918dce8-4f46-5431-aa05-6401af531bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563037} {:answer/id #uuid "caef0b1a-9245-5da8-b22a-8dda3c23c0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,1,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d7bfc2-51e1-5033-adbd-48343a267821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563028} :answer-field/id #uuid "88941887-29dc-54a4-8201-4203e16bb84e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05950ac2-f758-5682-b193-f12241b0a085" :question/math-academy-id "q-127820" :question/problem "Find $f(5)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(5)=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3{{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1681cfe9-8bfe-557b-bba9-dee04b551092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562993} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab6c84d-a691-5a1a-acd5-3b2973b35c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563042} {:answer/id #uuid "92fe21ff-aedd-582d-862d-28a80840405d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563274} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e97209-1671-561b-bcf4-837548575394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563339} {:answer/id #uuid "e6dc9c5c-0a08-5283-9a8c-3162c5f31765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ab6c84d-a691-5a1a-acd5-3b2973b35c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563042} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4af1a7d-f52e-5d64-bf12-4976f28c7f6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43d07e17-8355-5232-ab17-51baae273661" :question/math-academy-id "q-127855" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ is differentiable." :question/worked-solution "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one by one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{′}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1})}^{′}=\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is not differentiable at this points.\n\n- The derivative of  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is\n\n$$\n{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ except the points $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74401a99-8151-5848-bfcc-1eef2569c204" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval on Which a Product of Functions is Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d272829-2e53-5971-995f-8a13d9832bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563081} {:answer/id #uuid "6d31ed91-ba1f-5fb8-8b20-4a0b4f4638c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563194} {:answer/id #uuid "d39d0f66-0fea-55a8-95f1-96a73e042d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563407} {:answer/id #uuid "e1247620-39f5-51a2-a437-2fe0f4af181d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563442} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8f8ab7-5e9f-5a65-8f25-9e590ae8ecba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d31ed91-ba1f-5fb8-8b20-4a0b4f4638c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563194} :answer-field/id #uuid "816ea349-9124-5703-a18d-858815a67291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef76f5ac-70f5-5f95-86dd-2e2b577b8988" :question/math-academy-id "q-127893" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =|t-1{|}^{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{\\sqrt{t-1}}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $|t-1{|}^{3}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)$ is differentiable over $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(1,\\infty ).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable over $(-2,2).$\n\n- The second component ${e}^{\\sqrt{t-1}}$ is differentiable for $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{3}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(1,2).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is\n\n$$\n(1,\\infty )\\cap (1,2)=(1,2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86734a20-7239-53af-8c14-4b0571e0af66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113533} {:answer/id #uuid "db128928-7518-5c5b-8cfe-bf3d91157c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113535} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe392e-e1ac-52ad-b4f5-27498995f854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113536} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6c02a4-d696-5905-9ea0-e39e7c594871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113537} {:answer/id #uuid "c98a9e61-787a-5809-8c15-d98c6271b0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are continuous at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe392e-e1ac-52ad-b4f5-27498995f854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113536} :answer-field/id #uuid "02b0b012-b0b7-563a-8f4b-d1624ee864e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd56ae52-cc65-50ec-b35f-bed617e7fb7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-32292" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous at $t=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ h(t) & =2t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous at a point if and only if each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- Function $f(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ is not continuous at $t=0.$ Indeed, the left and right limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to +0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=+\\infty  \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to -0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ doesn’t exist.\n\n- Function $g(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\frac{2}{t}$ is not continuous at $t=0.$\n\n- Function $h(t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ because each of its components $2t$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and ${t}^{4}$ are continuous at $t=0.$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $h(t)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820033b9-1555-51ea-b385-5a8c06b808e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(t)$ and $r(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113593} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9e5225-8b8f-5ae6-8044-04a37abfb4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q(t)$ and $r(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113595} {:answer/id #uuid "b94bc508-8737-5b62-8438-131d4acb0c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(t)$ and $q(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113596} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc1a731-b484-519e-93a3-569ae5979606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the functions are differentiable on $t\\in (0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563043} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0eae6e-708e-5574-aa53-37eb5b37b2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are differentiable on $t\\in (0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f0eae6e-708e-5574-aa53-37eb5b37b2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are differentiable on $t\\in (0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563302} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e93cfcc-d1b2-57d7-a7be-139c5f3dd0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a626af8f-7439-5291-9587-816c4526871e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32698" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (0,1)?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =⟨2t-1,\\,{t}^{2}+4,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ q(t) & =⟨(t+1)^{4},\\,\\sqrt[3]{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3t+2,\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined on $t\\in (0,1).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in (0,1)$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(t+1)^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${q}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we have that $q(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $r(t)$ to find ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${r}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $r(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014d7fc8-5099-5882-8aa1-ded2fbbcf5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562941} {:answer/id #uuid "1662f69d-0d6b-5863-8f09-5d9d05419260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562992} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdede5d-fa26-5289-a238-94143dcae14e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563165} {:answer/id #uuid "9706168f-6882-59ce-be20-eefcba9f53d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563284} {:answer/id #uuid "dab073b4-14ff-58ad-bfaa-7282357cb131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1662f69d-0d6b-5863-8f09-5d9d05419260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562992} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c4782e-d988-5b6a-b328-68cc3dd0adb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d69b9419-ecda-5adb-b076-9a4d08de1698" :question/math-academy-id "q-32701" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨t\\sqrt{t},\\,1-{t}^{2},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$ if and only if each of its component functions is differentiable on that interval. \n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$ to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{1/2},\\,-2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t},\\,-2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of  ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we have that $g(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of  ${h}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we conclude that $h(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is: \n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54aee2c5-cc51-57ec-9a8a-f399690213ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563138} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd7361-80a7-5610-b411-db6d96b03f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,6,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563175} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1fb0b6-ee0d-51a1-80d9-cbcb788fb076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563257} {:answer/id #uuid "b02a670b-dc6a-56a6-bfc7-88e12bb41e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,3,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563338} {:answer/id #uuid "db46a840-c91e-50d2-8618-cbba1038d021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,8,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b02a670b-dc6a-56a6-bfc7-88e12bb41e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,3,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563338} :answer-field/id #uuid "42daf895-ed79-526d-aef4-19952bd37e0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a9c34c8-3d9e-57aa-9737-7f2bbf9076b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35104" :question/problem "Find $g(2)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(2)=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple, and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 2}2g(t) \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =f(2)+2g(2) \\\\  & ={{⟨1,1,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(2)}+2g(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,2⟩+2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =⟨-1,3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44b00ccb-8b01-596c-b4dc-2a3f3aa5a049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-3,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563099} {:answer/id #uuid "45c07133-7c78-580f-9ccc-4af3dc31a4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The value of $f(0)$ cannot be determined." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563102} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea82692-100a-5038-b8d4-644562b66a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563267} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1f037b-158b-5c79-8e25-3276bfa998c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563399} {:answer/id #uuid "f107cc53-ee96-570c-9ee3-a5f4ee8def54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f107cc53-ee96-570c-9ee3-a5f4ee8def54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563483} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fe35960-523d-5648-9c8e-2980e2ae45c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56eb1f68-e0b0-5d8c-82fe-65522b38bb34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35115" :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(0)=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-{{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74401a99-8151-5848-bfcc-1eef2569c204" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval on Which a Product of Functions is Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc46e27-71ea-59bf-b8a7-bb10f566c397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113768} {:answer/id #uuid "9f18fad1-1cd0-5b00-bdc0-9f0c14dfa40b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563303} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5c267b-63e8-5dd0-bb3a-f4eac717c321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563329} {:answer/id #uuid "c838c1be-6f79-573c-844f-4ea6d6563720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563386} {:answer/id #uuid "eafc1913-afb8-5c4d-969b-a557a69f332f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f18fad1-1cd0-5b00-bdc0-9f0c14dfa40b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563303} :answer-field/id #uuid "23bfd070-b01a-5822-ae32-7e8a483e1cb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "52e21306-1872-5a8d-b501-ce393b4c0493" :question/math-academy-id "q-35117" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =|t-1|\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{1-t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & ={t}^{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $|t-1|$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=1.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{1-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=1$ and the function is not defined for $t>1.$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,1).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component ${t}^{2}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}$ is differentiable for $(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)$ is differentiable only on $(-\\infty ,3).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,-3).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is $(-\\infty ,1)\\cap (-\\infty ,-3)=(-\\infty ,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9e5bb1-dd1e-56cc-a986-5db0a68be56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(t)$ and $r(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563041} {:answer/id #uuid "e0688584-1da5-5b8b-8b3f-e24c2240ab4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563437} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9caceb-1164-5695-8a9d-1fecd205232e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563470} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0f2c96-0b6d-51f6-9a90-39072da93530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the functions are differentiable on $t\\in [0,1]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563476} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bf877f-6b9d-5bf9-96b6-7245c5fd452f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(t)$ and $q(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea9caceb-1164-5695-8a9d-1fecd205232e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563470} :answer-field/id #uuid "19f0265f-4630-5691-88b4-b34a3b810e7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82aa3655-fc19-581e-ac6b-187bbf004ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-70480" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in [0,1]?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =⟨t-1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ q(t) & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}⟩ \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3t+2,\\,|t|,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in [0,1].$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in [0,1]$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one by one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $[0,1],$ we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{2t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{(2t-1)^{2}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the third component of this vector, $\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{(2t-1)^{2}}},$ is undefined at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that the function $q(t)$ is not differentiable on $[0,1].$\n\n- Finally, since the deviative of the second component $|t|$ of $r(t)$ is undefined at $t=0,$ we conclude that $r(t)$ is not differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"$p(t)$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d1dd38-790c-5ade-b6e6-f0c5c753a1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563001} {:answer/id #uuid "3eca838d-2fc1-5d4f-aaa8-60a40708145b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563086} {:answer/id #uuid "5aef3009-5a2d-508d-adca-eeca99616f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563149} {:answer/id #uuid "82573932-d8c9-5582-98a9-f1ce734bb0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563235} {:answer/id #uuid "a568051a-98f8-5b96-85bc-e0f25ae0b93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aef3009-5a2d-508d-adca-eeca99616f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563149} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a730784-58ce-5e73-830c-2d3bc30ec8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8698245e-3b42-575d-8a24-d13bd09126d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-70558" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,k$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one-by-one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded point $t=0.$\n\n- The function ${t}^{2}-1$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{t-2}$ is continuous everywhere except the point $t=2.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous everywhere except the points $t=0$ and $t=2.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d9530a-4fda-58ce-be9d-a68a94510ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,-3,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563056} {:answer/id #uuid "4befd6a6-4c2e-5f24-bda2-c235b60305c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,2,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563113} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c966e9-c274-5eab-aa26-39cd5b33e082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563325} {:answer/id #uuid "b472ddc0-a0fd-58d7-b0a3-868062a444c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨9,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563343} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f5e40e-4e6a-5985-b20a-99c944f7f9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨9,-3,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0f5e40e-4e6a-5985-b20a-99c944f7f9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨9,-3,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563403} :answer-field/id #uuid "f54036d0-b672-540d-9df5-0a5f88f5edb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1380f470-1cae-5a0e-b171-8e1423867447" :question/math-academy-id "q-70565" :question/problem "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-7)=⟨6,0,-2⟩$ and $g(-7)=⟨2,-1,0⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to -7}\\frac{f(t)}{2}+{lim}_{t\\to -7}3g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}f(-7)+3g(-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨6,0,-2⟩+3⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-1⟩+⟨6,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fa6bb7-d0f9-5e18-9b3c-e212aeb79600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562955} {:answer/id #uuid "29f5c5e8-ff02-5056-940b-e508ad765aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563040} {:answer/id #uuid "541e6159-1300-54d2-9ca7-82d337be42a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563135} {:answer/id #uuid "96a3c878-7b87-5ad8-a727-56d45e5d6afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563280} {:answer/id #uuid "f50708b8-5d3c-5122-8662-e4f8a58bf6f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29f5c5e8-ff02-5056-940b-e508ad765aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563040} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f7ff639-4ca5-57e6-a7d3-21f3b2dfb18a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22fc3cb9-9f8c-550e-b1a7-090b46545ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-70566" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-4}\\,j-5{t}^{2}\\,k$ is differentiable." :question/worked-solution "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one-by-one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{t}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{t})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=0,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{t}$ is not differentiable at this point.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{t-4})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(t-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $-5{t}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{(-5{t}^{2})}^{′}=-10t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-5{t}^{2}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ except the point $t=0,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0456835a-ab1e-5d54-96b5-8385b0418d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,-21,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562946} {:answer/id #uuid "23bce016-f53e-5fef-bcdf-fcb816c7238e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,21,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563025} {:answer/id #uuid "8400f582-fe6b-5605-9b73-4b03d4757ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-9,-3,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563241} {:answer/id #uuid "8c32fdb6-a254-566c-8621-94a8ef887edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563261} {:answer/id #uuid "fae49f8d-3748-58b3-8819-760de320ce89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,-7,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae49f8d-3748-58b3-8819-760de320ce89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,-7,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563507} :answer-field/id #uuid "36617721-cdaf-5e1b-9dc1-a68d20a6f48d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dae5e77-95b3-5116-bdbb-e15146f075a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71006" :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that $f(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(0)=3$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}u(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) \\\\  & =u(0)f(0) \\\\  & =3f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ 3f(0) & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-6,-7,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74401a99-8151-5848-bfcc-1eef2569c204" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval on Which a Product of Functions is Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093035b3-2936-55d6-8dab-3391d410df31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562956} {:answer/id #uuid "4f341999-9ba5-5f4a-9140-656c713efee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563126} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae917e7-8245-59fd-980b-c3a5c657ebed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563190} {:answer/id #uuid "ac377b35-816b-51bb-a07b-33e58ac30d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563335} {:answer/id #uuid "f93684b1-e617-524d-a9bc-88188148d840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac377b35-816b-51bb-a07b-33e58ac30d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563335} :answer-field/id #uuid "6735ac2c-1ab2-5f2e-8ebd-5ac1244d08a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48d56ec2-9a26-5561-ad14-109677da4c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-71088" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{t-3}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{3-t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{16-{t}^{2}}\\,i+{2}^{t-2}\\,j+({t}^{2}-4)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\frac{1}{t-3}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$, as it  is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{3-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,3)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=3$ and the function is not defined for $t>3.$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{16-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable on $(-4,4)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 4$ and the function is not defined for $|t|>4.$\n\n- The second component ${2}^{t-2}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{2}-4$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-4,4).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cap (-4,4)=(-4,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74401a99-8151-5848-bfcc-1eef2569c204" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval on Which a Product of Functions is Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062e3f00-c0cc-5c86-9d40-9b8fb8f5c06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562951} {:answer/id #uuid "0acff39c-0666-5bce-aa8a-12219d111a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562961} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4d0662-0e86-5867-95df-e87c9d2e0aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563049} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1e8518-dddd-5a84-83ff-fecff1302c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563156} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b0a692-d075-5ddc-a635-2f86c53a60c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0acff39c-0666-5bce-aa8a-12219d111a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562961} :answer-field/id #uuid "89f9c708-4753-5381-8ffa-d3d47ae26eb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef36aafd-d427-51c9-9cd0-af8bbd24e5b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-71089" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt[3]{t}\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j+(5+t)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt[3]{t}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at  $t=0.$\n\n- The second component ${e}^{2t}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $(5+t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is differentiable on $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is \n\n$$\n[(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )]\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74401a99-8151-5848-bfcc-1eef2569c204" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval on Which a Product of Functions is Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34dd1b4d-1762-57e5-a082-1e6af4b9595a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563064} {:answer/id #uuid "93f606fe-6d18-58e7-bbf9-e3cdd3fe5e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563275} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f808e0-fb9c-5164-9715-84ca77aa6cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563373} {:answer/id #uuid "d78598a0-c8b4-585e-b759-394d69f24982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563418} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a768fd-bbcd-5923-b417-ba559141df95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34dd1b4d-1762-57e5-a082-1e6af4b9595a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563064} :answer-field/id #uuid "9188640e-0ced-51d7-bb85-9890813bb8e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cff5ef5d-ae0d-5005-af50-0efa2caca50a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71094" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={t}^{2}+2 \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt[3]{t-3}\\,i+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where both $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. The vector function $g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where its components are differentiable.  \n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. \n\nFor the real-valued function $u(t):$  \n\n- We observe that $u(t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component, $\\sqrt[3]{t-3}$, is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$ as its derivative is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second and third components, ${cos}^{2}⁡t$ and $\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}},$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the domains of all three components of  $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$  \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$"}]}
